# F1 router-mode drive — PER-USER container secrets template.
#   cp .env.user.example .env.user
# `.env.user` is gitignored; this `.env.user.example` is committed as documentation.
# Runbook: apps/f1/test-drives/README-router-mode.md
#
# SCOPE: these are NOT env vars for the router-server process. They are one
# user's secrets, seeded into the router's SecretStore (<home>/secrets.json)
# via `./f1 router:seed-user`, and injected by the router into THAT USER's
# issue containers at boot (spread into the container env, minus reserved
# keys). Router/control-plane config lives in `.env` (see .env.example).
# Simulating multiple users? Keep one copy per user (e.g. .env.user.alice,
# .env.user.bob — `**/.env.*` is gitignored).
#
# These values are referenced on the SHELL command line, so source both files
# before seeding, from apps/f1:
#   set -a && source .env && source .env.user && set +a
#   ./f1 router:seed-user \
#     --email you@example.com \
#     --linear-id lin-you \
#     --claude-token "$CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN" \
#     --env LINEAR_API_TOKEN="$LINEAR_API_TOKEN" \
#     --env GIT_TOKEN="$GIT_TOKEN" \
#     --env GIT_USER_NAME="$GIT_USER_NAME" \
#     --env GIT_USER_EMAIL="$GIT_USER_EMAIL"
#
# Rotation caveat: a container captures its env at boot. Re-seeding a secret
# only affects the next FRESH boot — destroy the issue's container first to
# apply immediately.
#
# Reserved keys (rejected by the store; router-controlled): CYRUS_ROUTER_URL,
# CYRUS_DEVICE_TOKEN, CYRUS_ISSUE_KEY, CYRUS_REPOS_JSON, CYRUS_WORKSPACES_DIR,
# CYRUS_REPO_CACHE_DIR, PATH, HOME, NODE_OPTIONS.

# Real Claude Code OAuth token (get one with: claude setup-token). Required for
# a user whose default runner is Claude — which is the default when no runner
# has been picked — and the router blocks their boot without it.
#
# NOT required for a user seeded with `--default-runner codex:<model>`: since
# NOR-364 the required-key set is a function of the chosen runner, and a Codex
# user authenticates with a router-held ChatGPT subscription credential
# instead (see CODEX_AUTH below). Leave this blank when driving that path —
# seeding it anyway hides the very gate the drive is checking.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Linear token — a Personal API Key or pre-obtained OAuth access token
# (interactive OAuth is NOT possible inside a container). When seeded, the
# container's Claude session gets the hosted Linear MCP authenticated with it.
LINEAR_API_TOKEN=

# GitHub token. GH_TOKEN is the canonical one: `gh` reads it natively, and
# `gh auth setup-git` makes git ride the same credential — so ONE variable
# authenticates cloning, pushing AND `gh pr create`.
#
# GIT_TOKEN is the legacy git-only fallback (a manual ~/.git-credentials).
# It clones and pushes but CANNOT open a pull request, because `gh` never sees
# it — a session seeded with GIT_TOKEN alone gets as far as a pushed branch and
# then stops. Seed GH_TOKEN unless you specifically want that older path.
GH_TOKEN=
GIT_TOKEN=

# Git identity for commits made inside the container.
# QUOTE values containing spaces (this file is `source`d by your shell):
#   GIT_USER_NAME="Ada Lovelace"
GIT_USER_NAME=
GIT_USER_EMAIL=

# Codex subscription credential, for a user whose default runner is Codex.
# Not seeded as an --env var: it is sealed on the users row rather than stored
# in the secret bundle (ADR 0005), so it is passed by PATH:
#   ./f1 router:seed-user ... --default-runner codex:gpt-5.5 \
#     --codex-auth ~/.codex/auth.json
# Produce the file with `codex login --device-auth` on your own machine. The
# router refreshes it and injects a fresh short-lived copy as CODEX_AUTH_JSON
# at each container boot; the container never refreshes on its own.
#
# Metered alternative, if you would rather not connect a subscription:
#   --env OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...

# Optional: per-user dotfiles repo; its install.sh runs at container boot.
# DOTFILES_REPO=
